### Description

I did report this to MSRC but the report was closed saying "We determined that this behavior is considered to be by design. This behavior is due to how the GC and the runtime works. The CryptoStream is actually making a copy of the data internal which the developer doesn't control and cannot clear. The ArrayPool after clearing the original buffer actual returns the internal buffer CryptoStream copied the data into.". So they said it isn't a vulnerability but I still thought it was unexpected. I would have expected that once my data was encrypted using CryptoStream that my unencrypted data would not be available to rent via ArrayPool. 

### Reproduction Steps

```csharp
// Simulate getting the data from somewhere and let's say it gets the word "secret" encoded in UTF-8. 
var data = new byte []{ 0x73, 0x65, 0x63, 0x72, 0x65, 0x74 };

// Ensure we dispose of streams before we try to rent from the array pool.
using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
{
    using (var cs = new CryptoStream(ms, Aes.Create().CreateEncryptor(), CryptoStreamMode.Write))
    {
        cs.Write(data);
        cs.FlushFinalBlock();
    }
   // Do something with encrypted data in memory stream.
}
// Ensure my data is cleared from memory after being encrypted.
Array.Clear(data);

var rental = ArrayPool<byte>.Shared.Rent(data.Length);

// The line below outputs: Rented from the pool and got secret
Console.WriteLine($"Rented from the pool and got {Encoding.UTF8.GetString(rental)}");
```

### Expected behavior

Renting from the array pool after using CryptoStream would not return my unencrypted data.

### Actual behavior

Renting from the array pool after using CryptoStream returns my unencrypted data.

